<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dương Đình Nghệ</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/937">937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Duong Dinh Nghe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dương Đình Nghệ was a Vietnamese military leader who expelled the Southern Han forces from the Red River delta in 931 and governed it as an autonomous military governor of Tĩnh Hải circuit. He was murdered in 937 by his officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kieu-cong-tien">Kiều Công Tiễn</a>, whose call for Southern Han help was answered by Ngô Quyền, Dương's son-in-law, at Bạch Đằng in 938.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dương Đình Nghệ was born in 874 in Ái Châu, modern Thanh Hóa. Some sources, among them Chinese works such as the History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and the Zizhi Tongjian, write his name Dương Diên Nghệ, a confusion of two similar-looking characters. He grew up as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> declined. Around 905 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuc-thua-du">Khúc Thừa Dụ</a> had taken the office of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiedushi">jiedushi</a> of Tĩnh Hải quân, beginning native rule, and Dương served three generations of the Khúc clan as a swordsman and commander, an esteemed general under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuc-hao">Khúc Hạo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 919 Khúc Thừa Mỹ sought investiture from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-liang">Later Liang</a>, angering the Southern Han, whose king Liu Yan sent his general Li Ke Zheng against Tĩnh Hải quân in 923; Chinese sources say 930. Khúc Thừa Mỹ was captured and taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and the Southern Han installed Lý Tiến as administrator, with a large army in the fortified garrison of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dai-la">Đại La</a>. In Ái Châu Dương gathered 3,000 warriors whom he regarded as adopted sons, training them at wrestling grounds he set up.</p><p>In 931 he marched north and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dai-la">Đại La</a>. Lý Tiến fled back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>; Dương took the fortress, killed the relief commander Trình Bảo in a counter-attack and drove the remaining Chinese forces to the border. He cultivated a circle of young commanders, among them Ngô Quyền, who married his daughter Dương Thị Ngọc.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He was murdered in March 937. After his death the Dương clan kept an indirect influence on politics: Ngô Quyền, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinh-tien-hoang">Đinh Bộ Lĩnh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-dai-hanh">Lê Hoàn</a> all married women of the clan, and these women played crucial roles in deciding the succession. The historian Keith Weller Taylor contrasts him with Khúc Thừa Mỹ, who pursued an idealised vision of Tang civilisation: Dương was ready to use practical political power and, by building local authority against the Southern Han, paved the way for Vietnamese national consciousness. He is counted among the national heroes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dương Đình Nghệ” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 938 Ngo Quyen, son-in-law of <b>Duong Dinh Nghe</b>, defeated the Southern Han navy on the Bach Dang river by planting iron-tipped stakes in the riverbed and luring the fleet onto them at low tide.</blockquote><a href="/state/ngo-dynasty">Ngo dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Duong Dinh Nghe</b> expelled the Southern Han in 931 but was murdered by Kieu Cong Tien in 937.</blockquote><a href="/state/tinh-hai-circuit">Tinh Hai Circuit</a></li></ul></section>
